# The build context is the WORKSPACE ROOT now, not a single component directory.
#
# That is the whole point of the migration's image change — the Dockerfiles COPY prebuilt
# output instead of pulling published tarballs from a registry — but it has a consequence
# nobody has to think about in a single-component repository: without this file, `docker
# build -f frontend/ui/Dockerfile .` sends the ENTIRE workspace to the daemon. Measured here:
# 1.9 GB in the root node_modules alone, plus a per-package node_modules for all 33 members,
# every dist tree, .nx, and a 125 MB .git.
#
# Everything an image needs is either a build output (frontend/*/dist, and the pnpm deploy
# tree under .deploy/) or a small static asset (nginx config), so the exclusions can be broad
# and the inclusions specific.

# Dependencies — the images either COPY a `pnpm deploy --prod` tree or need nothing at all.
**/node_modules

# Version control and CI machinery
.git
.github
.nx

# Everything not needed to assemble an image
migration
tools/migration
tests
**/*.md
**/.storybook
**/dist-showcase
**/__image_snapshots__
**/__diff_output__
**/coverage
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.log

# Source. The images copy build OUTPUT; shipping sources would bloat the context and, for
# any image that later runs an install, risk building from them by accident.
**/src
**/test
**/tests

# Re-include what the two Dockerfiles actually read. Order matters: a later ! overrides an
# earlier exclusion, and .deploy is gitignored but must reach the daemon.
#
# The `/**` suffixes are load-bearing and their absence cost a run. `!.deploy` alone
# re-includes the DIRECTORY ENTRY and nothing beneath it, so `**/node_modules` above still
# excluded .deploy/build-task-consumer/node_modules and the build failed with
# "/.deploy/build-task-consumer/node_modules": not found.
#
# The Verify step had already printed, three lines earlier:
#
#     OK      .deploy/build-task-consumer/node_modules  (16258 files)
#
# which is exactly what that guard is for. On disk and in the build context are two different
# questions, and without the guard "not found" is ambiguous between "never built" and
# "excluded from the context" — two very different bugs that read identically.
!frontend/*/dist
!frontend/*/dist/**
!frontend/*/packages/*/dist
!frontend/*/packages/*/dist/**
!frontend/ui/nginx
!frontend/ui/nginx/**
!.deploy
!.deploy/**
